                    r1_tag: `DOSSIER`,
                    r1_title: `Round 1 — Appraise the Evidence`,
                    r1_brief: `On 6 May 2026 CardioNova filed the <strong>VentriGuard</strong> dossier — an implantable monitoring-and-therapy device for advanced heart failure at <strong>€22,000/patient</strong>. The case rests on a <strong>single sponsor-funded RCT (n=480, 12-month follow-up)</strong> claiming a <strong>0.6 QALY gain</strong> and a 22% cut in rehospitalizations, but with wide confidence intervals and no independent or long-term data. Before you judge the device, set the rules of the game: what <strong>willingness-to-pay threshold</strong> per QALY will you apply, what <strong>evidence standard</strong> must the dossier clear, and which <strong>QALY case</strong> will you carry into the model.`,
                    r1_coach: `“Tarricone's point is that device evidence needs HTA discipline, not clinician enthusiasm. If you accept the headline 0.6 QALY at face value, you have already lost the appraisal. Set your threshold and your evidence bar <em>before</em> you see whether the device passes — that is what makes the verdict defensible.”`,

                    r2_tag: `MODEL`,
                    r2_title: `Round 2 — Run the HTA: ICER & Budget Impact`,
                    r2_brief: `Now build the core analysis. Incremental cost per patient is roughly <strong>€18,000</strong> (device minus standard care). At the trial's 0.6 QALY the ICER is about <strong>€34,000/QALY</strong> — inside the band; halve the benefit and it jumps to <strong>€68,000/QALY</strong>. But cost-effectiveness is not affordability: ~150 eligible patients here scale to <strong>~1,400 regionally</strong> because your verdict sets a precedent. Define the <strong>eligible sub-group</strong>, the expected <strong>uptake</strong>, and whether you model <strong>hospital or regional</strong> scale.`,
                    r2_coach: `“A device can be cost-effective per patient and still bankrupt the system. The advanced sub-group is where the QALYs concentrate; open it to everyone and you maximise budget impact while diluting value. Watch the regional budget-impact figure — that is the money you would take from oncology, diabetes and emergency care.”`,

                    r3_tag: `VERDICT`,
                    r3_title: `Round 3 — The Recommendation & Reimbursement Tier`,
                    r3_brief: `Issue the three core decisions. Choose an <strong>adoption recommendation</strong> (adopt / adopt-with-restrictions / managed-access / reject), then the <strong>reimbursement tier</strong> that controls spend and risk (full / capped volume / outcomes-based rebate / co-payment), and finally a hard <strong>volume cap</strong>. A tier with no volume control is hope, not budget management. Tie the spend to the value you actually expect to receive.`,
                    r3_coach: `“Hope is not a budget control. If the cardiology service implants 150 this year and 250 the next, who pays? A price-volume cap, an outcomes-based rebate or a co-payment turns your reimbursement tier into a risk-management instrument. And remember — managed access lets you adopt conditionally while the evidence matures, rather than an all-or-nothing yes/no.”`,

                    r4_tag: `CHALLENGE`,
                    r4_title: `Round 4 — Defend & Negotiate`,
                    r4_brief: `The challenge round. The <strong>cardiology chief</strong> contests your restriction and threatens the medical board and the press. The <strong>vendor</strong> offers a confidential discount or an outcomes-based deal. A <strong>patient group</strong> demands wider access. Decide whether to <strong>hold the line, re-tier, or negotiate a managed-access agreement</strong> — without breaching the budget — then defend the verdict to the regional HTA board on ICER, budget impact, QALYs and access.`,
                    r4_coach: `“If HTA verdicts bend to the loudest clinician, the framework is theatre. Hold a defensible position, but keep the door open through managed access and tie any concession to the vendor's discount or an outcomes-based contract. Caving here undoes three rounds of disciplined analysis.”`,

                    d_wtp_label: `Willingness-to-pay threshold (€/QALY)`,
                    d_wtp_hint: `The cost-per-QALY ceiling the device must clear. The commonly cited band is €25,000–40,000.`,
