                    d_equity_label: `Patient-group access demand`,
                    d_equity_hint: `An advocacy group demands wider access for moderate-risk patients.`,
                    d_equity_hold: `Hold eligibility to the value-rich sub-group`,
                    d_equity_ced: `Open via coverage-with-evidence-development`,
                    d_equity_open: `Open access broadly to satisfy the demand`,

                    fb_r1_face: `<strong>You swallowed the dossier.</strong> Carrying the 0.6-QALY trial figure at face value means your whole case rests on one 12-month sponsor-funded study. Halve that benefit — well within the confidence interval — and the ICER doubles toward €68,000/QALY, outside any defensible threshold. Did you set an evidence standard the dossier actually clears?`,
                    fb_r1_strong: `<strong>Disciplined appraisal.</strong> You set a real evidence bar and tested the pessimistic case rather than the sponsor's headline. That is exactly the critical-appraisal posture device HTA requires — surrogate endpoints, sponsorship bias and generalizability to frailer real-world patients all matter.`,
                    fb_r1_mixed: `<strong>Partial discipline.</strong> You set a threshold but leaned on optimistic assumptions or a weak evidence standard. The dossier's wide confidence intervals mean the benefit could be much smaller than claimed — make the pessimistic case part of your test, not an afterthought.`,

                    fb_r2_all: `<strong>Value diluted.</strong> Opening eligibility to all HF admissions maximises budget impact while minimising value — the trial benefit concentrated in advanced patients, so moderate-risk patients carry a far worse ICER. Which patients actually get the QALYs?`,
                    fb_r2_budget: `<strong>Affordability shock.</strong> Your modelled regional budget impact exceeds what the system can absorb. A technology can be cost-effective per patient and still be unaffordable in aggregate — that is the central HTA tension, and the money would come from oncology, diabetes and emergency care.`,
                    fb_r2_targeted: `<strong>Value-targeted.</strong> Concentrating on the advanced sub-group keeps the ICER favourable and the budget impact contained. Now make sure the reimbursement tier follows the same line you have drawn here.`,
                    fb_r2_mixed: `<strong>Reasonable model.</strong> Your sub-group and uptake assumptions are plausible, but check the regional figure: the precedent effect scales the eligible pool roughly nine-fold, and that is where affordability bites.`,

                    fb_r3_blanket: `<strong>Cost-effective but unaffordable.</strong> Full unrestricted adoption with no reimbursement controls is the classic error — an ICER inside the threshold does not make €30M+ a year affordable. Where is the volume cap, rebate or co-payment that ties spend to value?`,
                    fb_r3_reject: `<strong>Defensible, but value left on the table.</strong> Rejecting on immature evidence is reasonable — but you have forfeited a real clinical benefit and handed the cardiology chief a press story. Tarricone's point is that uncertainty can be managed, not only refused. Would coverage-with-evidence-development capture value while the data matures?`,
                    fb_r3_managed: `<strong>Risk-managed access.</strong> A restricted or managed-access verdict with a real reimbursement mechanism turns your tier into a budget-control instrument. This is value-based procurement done properly: adopt conditionally, tie spend to value, and define what evidence triggers full adoption.`,
                    fb_r3_mixed: `<strong>Incomplete design.</strong> Your recommendation and tier do not yet fully cohere — make sure the eligibility line, the reimbursement mechanism and the volume control all point the same way.`,

                    fb_r4_cave: `<strong>The framework became theatre.</strong> The chief threatened the board and the press, and your recommendation loosened. If HTA verdicts bend to the loudest clinician, the analysis was decoration. A defensible no — with a managed-access door left open — is how you say no to a powerful colleague.`,
                    fb_r4_strong: `<strong>Held the line, managed the uncertainty.</strong> You defended the framework under pressure and tied any concession to an outcomes-based agreement. That keeps the door open to a real clinical benefit while protecting the budget — governance independence with a managed-entry middle path.`,
                    fb_r4_inconsistent: `<strong>Inconsistent posture.</strong> Declining the vendor's risk-sharing offer while opening access broadly maximises both cost and exposure. If you widen eligibility, you need the discount or outcomes-based deal to keep the budget impact defensible.`,
                    fb_r4_mixed: `<strong>Defensible verdict.</strong> You held a reasonable position. Tighten the link between the concession you made and the mechanism that controls its cost — that is what the board will probe.`,

                    hint_icer_over: `Your ICER is above the threshold you set. Either tighten eligibility to the value-rich sub-group, negotiate a discount, or revisit your QALY assumption.`,
                    hint_budget_high: `Regional budget impact is beyond what the system can absorb — cost-effective per patient is not the same as affordable. Add a volume cap or restrict eligibility.`,
                    hint_evidence_weak: `Your evidence discipline is thin. Test the pessimistic QALY case and demand independent or long-term data before approving.`,
